      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the probability that the first card drawn is a 6 and the second card drawn is a Queen from a standard deck of 52 cards, we can break the problem into two parts: finding the probability of drawing a 6 first, and then finding the probability of drawing a Queen next given that a 6 has been drawn in the first place.\n\n1. **Probability of drawing a 6 first:**\n   There are 4 sixes in a standard deck of 52 cards (one for each suit: hearts, clubs, spades, and diamonds). So, the probability of drawing a 6 first is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{52} = \\frac{1}{13}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Probability of drawing a Queen second after a 6 has been drawn first:**\n   After one card is drawn, the deck has 51 cards left. There are still 4 Queens in the deck (one for each suit). So, the probability of drawing a Queen second, given that a 6 has been drawn first, is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Second card is a Queen} \\mid \\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{51}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Combined probability:**\n   Since these events are independent, the combined probability of both events happening (drawing a 6 first and then a Queen) is the product of their individual probabilities:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{First card is a 6 and second card is a Queen}) = P(\\text{First card is a 6}) \\times P(\\text{Second card is a Queen} \\mid \\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{1}{13} \\times \\frac{4}{51} = \\frac{4}{663}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that the first card is a 6 and the second card is a Queen is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{4}{663}}\\).",
